nhtsa-vehicle pack: content licence
===================================

This pack is derived from the three US National Highway Traffic Safety
Administration (NHTSA) and Federal Register sources listed below (S1 to S3 of
the source-vetting record). Their landing-page URLs are held in the build
record dated 2026-09-25 and are not reproduced here; the repository link
policy bans source-material hosts (see ../../docs/LICENSING.md).

Source licence: Public Domain (US Government work, 17 U.S.C. 105)  (tier 1; see
../../docs/SOURCE-VETTING.md). No source-material download link is published.

All three sources are works of the United States Government prepared by NHTSA
and Office of the Federal Register staff, and are NOT subject to copyright
protection in the United States (17 U.S.C. 105). They are in the public
domain: anyone may reproduce, transform, and redistribute them for any
purpose, including commercially, without permission and without any
attribution or share-alike obligation.

Attribution to NHTSA is provided as a courtesy (see the repository NOTICE),
not as a legal obligation. NHTSA does not endorse this pack or its authors.

Each source below carries a vetting block: exact title, publisher office,
pinned date, status, publication citation, licence basis, what the pack
reconstructs from it, and the chapters that use it. Items marked "confirmed
from PDF" were checked against the local source-text captures on 2026-09-25.

Reconstruction: all pack content is synthesized reference notes in the authors'
own words. The documents themselves are not republished here; quotations are
limited to short passages (two-sentence cap per quote, enforced by the pack
gates).

NOTE: This file governs the CONTENT of this pack. The repository tooling is
licensed separately under MIT (see ../../LICENSE).


THE THREE SOURCES
=================

S1  Cybersecurity Best Practices for the Safety of Modern Vehicles, Updated 2022
    Publisher: US DOT National Highway Traffic Safety Administration
    Final guidance   Issued: September 2022 (publication code 15745-090822-v2a
    on the last page; 25 PDF pages, confirmed from PDF)
    Publication citation: notice published in the Federal Register at 87 FR
    55459, document 2022-19507, published 2022-09-09; docket NHTSA-2020-0087
    Status: Final. A docket re-check on 2026-09-25 (regulations.gov API and
    Federal Register API) found no newer final. This is the 2022 final, not
    the 2016 draft-era document (DOT HS 812 333, "Cybersecurity Best Practices
    for Modern Vehicles"), confirmed from the cover page, the "2022 Update
    Release Notes", and the enumerated-practice convention.
    Basis: US Government work, 17 U.S.C. 105
    Reconstructs: the 45 general best practices ([G.1] to [G.45], confirmed
    from PDF) and 25 technical best practices ([T.1] to [T.25], confirmed from
    PDF): leadership priority for product cybersecurity, the vehicle
    development process with explicit cybersecurity risk assessment, inventory
    and management of hardware and software assets, cybersecurity testing and
    monitoring, information sharing (Auto-ISAC), security vulnerability
    reporting, organizational incident response, self-auditing, education,
    aftermarket and user-owned devices, serviceability, and the technical
    controls (debug access, cryptography, diagnostics, internal communications,
    event logs, wireless paths, software and over-the-air updates).
    Used in: ch02, ch03, ch04

S2  Automated Driving Systems 2.0: A Vision for Safety (DOT HS 812 442)
    Publisher: US DOT National Highway Traffic Safety Administration
    Voluntary guidance   Issued: September 2017   Status: still current per the
    Federal Register notice of 2026-07-31 (programme research)
    Basis: US Government work, 17 U.S.C. 105
    Reconstructs: the voluntary guidance structure for automated driving
    systems: SAE International's levels of driving automation as adopted and
    described in the document, the 12 priority safety design elements (system
    safety; operational design domain; object and event detection and
    response; fallback (minimal risk condition); validation methods; human
    machine interface; vehicle cybersecurity; crashworthiness; post-crash ADS
    behavior; data recording; consumer education and training; and federal,
    state, and local laws), and the Voluntary Safety Self-Assessment. Its Best Practices
    section covers Federal and State roles, including best practices for
    legislatures and for state highway safety officials. SAE J3016 itself is
    copyrighted; this pack restates only what the NHTSA document says and
    names the standard.
    Used in: ch05, ch06

S3  49 CFR Part 571 selections: Federal Motor Vehicle Safety Standards (FMVSS)
    Publisher: Office of the Federal Register (eCFR)   Regulation
    Text currency: GovInfo annual edition 2024 (CFR-2024-title49-vol6, Part
    571; 959 PDF pages), retrieved 2026-09-25 via GovInfo
    Basis: US Government work, 17 U.S.C. 105
    Reconstructs: a landscape of obligations, not a republication: the Subpart
    A general framework (scope, definitions, applicability, incorporation by
    reference, and the manufacturer self-certification duty) plus the selected
    standards named in the build record: 101 (controls and displays), 105 and
    135 (brake systems), 111 (rear visibility), 124 (accelerator control
    systems), 126 and 136 (electronic stability control systems), 138 (tire
    pressure monitoring systems), and 141 (minimum sound requirements for
    hybrid and electric vehicles), stated as named obligations with their
    engineering intent. Full standard bodies are not dumped into the pack.
    Used in: ch01, ch07


EXCLUDED MATERIAL (cited by name only; no text reproduced)
=========================================================

The following standards and regulations are NOT licensed to this pack. NHTSA
guidance and the FMVSS reference them; the pack names them and restates the
agency's own recognition and discussion text only. It reproduces no standard
text, table, requirement, or clause wording from any of them.

-   ISO 26262 series, "Road vehicles: Functional safety" (2018, parts 1 to 12),
    and its base standard IEC 61508, "Functional safety of electrical/
    electronic/programmable electronic safety-related systems" (2010, parts 1
    to 7). Paywalled; cited by name only, including in the ch08 orientation.
-   ISO 21448:2022, "Road vehicles: Safety of the intended functionality"
    (SOTIF). Paywalled; cited by name only.
-   ISO/SAE 21434:2021, "Road vehicles: Cybersecurity engineering". Paywalled;
    named by the S1 source itself and cited by name only here.
-   SAE J3016_202104, "Taxonomy and Definitions for Terms Related to Driving
    Automation Systems". Paywalled; ADS 2.0 adopts its levels, and this pack
    names it without reproducing it.
-   UL 4600, "Standard for Evaluation of Autonomous Products" (Ed 3, 2023).
    Paywalled; cited by name only.
-   Automotive SPICE PRA/PAM v4.0 (VDA QMC, 2023). Free to download but
    (c) VDA QMC with no-derivatives terms; cited by name only.
-   UNECE Regulation No. 155 (cyber security and cyber security management
    system) and Regulation No. 156 (software update and software updates
    management system). Free to download but (c) UNECE; citation only, never
    full text.

These exclusions and the cleared status of S1 to S3 are recorded in
../../docs/SOURCE-VETTING.md. Buy copyrighted items from ISO, SAE, UL, IEC, or
VDA QMC as applicable; the automotive-signpost pack carries the automotive
standards pointer table and the functional-safety-signpost pack carries the
functional-safety pointer table, including the free-download paths.
